          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed" set:html={`Most families benefit from having both. A midwife manages your clinical care and delivery. A doula provides continuous emotional and physical support throughout labor. Research shows that births with both a midwife and a doula have the lowest intervention rates and highest satisfaction scores. If budget is a concern, prioritize the clinical provider your situation requires, then explore sliding-scale or community doula programs. See our <a href="/doula-cost/" class="text-tjb-rose-500 hover:text-tjb-rose-600 transition">doula cost guide</a> for affordable options.`} />
        </div>
        <div class="border-b border-tjb-lavender-300/40 pb-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">Can a doula deliver a baby?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">No. A doula cannot deliver a baby. Only a licensed medical provider like a midwife, OB, or physician can manage a delivery. A doula supports you through labor but does not perform any clinical task, including cervical checks, fetal monitoring, or catching the baby.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">Does insurance cover a doula?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed" set:html={`Midwifery care is widely covered by insurance, especially Certified Nurse-Midwives in hospital settings. Doula services are often out-of-pocket, though some Medicaid programs and private insurers now cover doula care. Check your plan directly, and see our guide to <a href="/medicaid-doula-coverage/" class="text-tjb-rose-500 hover:text-tjb-rose-600 transition">Medicaid doula coverage</a> for state-specific details.`} />
        </div>
      </div>

